      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-02-12T15:30:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah - that\u0027s a good question. I tested this out manually and it does throw a 500 with this patch. I retested with master and it still throws a 500 because I didn\u0027t have any certificates setup for PKI. I checked out the change before that refactor happened and ran `keystone-manage pki_setup` [0]. Once I had the certificates installed, I got the intended response from the API [1]. I think what we can do instead is to return an empty list instead of calling to get a list of revoked tokens from the backend. Or, we could make the logic conditional. If the provider supports fetching revoked tokens, then we can call it and expect a list. If not, then we return an empty list.\n\nWhen I removed this logic and replaced it with an empty list [2], the API response was the same as when it called the _persistence backend for a list of revoked tokens.\n\n[0] https://github.com/openstack/keystone/commit/928d23db022157ae24d49eb9b7772cc0e07345a4#diff-41c8207a4e9c66c80ef1576614634992\n[1] http://paste.openstack.org/raw/670054/\n[2] http://paste.openstack.org/raw/670097/",
      "parentUuid": "3fa0c359_6685db59",
